  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1250 AM CDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nNear term through tonight: Dry trend for today continues. For most\nof the day, organized showers and storms will have hard time making\nit even into southern WI as gradient of instability and convergence\nfrom low-level jet remain south. Moisture advection on north edge\ncould result in a few showers over far southern areas, but most \nlocations will stay dry through late afternoon. Even so, mid clouds\nwill hold down temps in the upper 70s to near 80. A few more showers\nand maybe a storm could develop over central WI late this afternoon\nas lift from shortwave moves in from central plains and interacts\nwith CAPEs up to 500J/kg along a warm front. Better chances for \nshowers and isolated storms occurs this evening into the overnight\nas tail of shortwave over Lake Superior and cold front cross the \nstate. Right entrance region of jet will also help organize \nshowers, especially over northern WI, where some rain is certainly\nneeded. Even as PWATs near 1.8 inches (95+ percentile) will be \ninteracting with the forcing, rainfall amounts look on the light \nside with minimal instability. If a heavier swath of showers \ndevelops it could produce over 0.5 inch of rain over north-central\nWI, but that prospect looks quite localized. Signal is there that\nnot much rain at all makes it into northeast or east-central WI \nwhere HREF means showing a min in QPF. \n\nPop-up storm chances Sunday: Despite being behind the front on\nSunday, shortwave dragging through during peak heating may kick \noff isolated showers and storms over from Door County to along the\nlakeshore in the early afternoon. Not a lot for pops (20-30%), \nbut CAPEs over 1000J/kg boosted by lingering higher dewpoints \nand effective shear over 30 kts could produce a stronger storm.\n\nNext chance for rain is Tuesday into Wednesday: Sharpening northwest\nflow pushes stronger shortwave and cold front across the western\nGreat Lakes region. Though forecast instability and various\nMachine Learning output suggest highest signal stays southwest\nfrom southern MN into IA, it will not take much increase in CAPE\nto lead to some severe threat across WI as the forcing arrives \nduring peak heating on Tuesday afternoon. Uncertainty on how fast\nthis system exits and models are not surprisingly showing some\nvariability with that. Overall, other than tonight's shower\nchances, this system looks like the greatest opportunity for \nrain next week. Cooler air push behind this system remains on track,\nwith highs settling into the lower to middle 70s Wednesday through\nFriday.",
